PHASE 3 -- BREAK IN AND GRAB THE FILE
═════════════════════════════════════
tool:  metasploit   (you open it by typing: msfconsole)

Recap: phase 1 found the way in (the exposed jenkins-old box).
Phase 2 confirmed it's unlocked (CVE-2018-1000861 -- a hole that
lets you in with no password). This step actually climbs through
and takes what we came for.

The file we want is the IC memo, sitting on a drive mounted to the
Jenkins box:

    /mnt/exec/ic-memo.txt

metasploit is the break-in tool. It keeps a ready-made attack for
almost every known hole; you tell it which machine to hit and it
does the break-in for you. We've already loaded the attack for our
hole -- you just aim it and fire.


─── do this ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Type these one line at a time:

    msfconsole
    set RHOSTS jenkins-old.internal.crazy.ants
    exploit
    cat /mnt/exec/ic-memo.txt

In plain terms: open the tool, aim it at the Jenkins box, break in,
then read the memo. After `exploit` the prompt goes blank -- that's
the break-in working. Every command you type now runs directly on
the Jenkins box, as the jenkins user. `cat /mnt/exec/ic-memo.txt`
opens the memo and the story takes over. Press Enter through the
screens, then type `continue` to close out m0usunet.


─── for next time: the full pattern ──────────────────────────────

Every metasploit break-in is the same six steps:

    search        find the ready-made attack by its CVE id
    use           select it
    show options  see what it needs from you
    set           fill those in (which machine, where to call back)
    check         make sure the target is really vulnerable
    exploit       fire; if it works, you're in

We did search / use / show / check for you. You did set + exploit.
Same muscles, fewer reps.

Stuck?  cat hint  -- no penalty.
